      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gore%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Gore changed its name on October 22, 1909, in honor of Oklahoma Senator Thomas Gore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gore%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Tahlonteskee, the capital of the Western Cherokee from 1828 to 1839, was located just two miles north of Gore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gore%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The settlement of Gore grew along a railroad route that was later leased to the Saint Louis, Iron Mountain and Southern Railway.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
